\fs96 \cf2 Errors, error handling, and context managers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo represents errors as values\'97specifically, as alternate return values from functions. Unlike stack-unwinding exceptions in languages like C++ or Java, Mojo errors don't require expensive call stack unwinding, so their runtime overhead is as low as returning and checking an extra 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This design also enables error handling in contexts where traditional exceptions aren't available, like GPU kernels.\
This page covers:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Raise an error\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Use the built-in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type to raise errors with string messages.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Handle an error\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to detect and recover from errors.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Typed errors\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Define custom error types as structs for structured error data and compile-time type checking.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Representing multiple error conditions\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Use enumerated error types or the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type for pattern matching.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul The 
\fs28 \cb12 Never
\fs32 \cb1  type\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Mark functions that always raise or never raise.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Parametric raises\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Write generic functions that propagate error types from their arguments.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Typed and 
\fs28 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cb1  interaction\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Work with code that uses both error styles.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Stack traces\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Enable stack trace collection for debugging.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Context managers\cf2 \ulnone  \'97 Manage resources safely with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 An error interrupts the normal execution flow of your program. If you provide an error handler (using 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 try
\fs32 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \ulnone ) in the current function, execution resumes with that handler. If the error isn't handled in the current function, it propagates to the calling function, and so on. If an error isn't caught by any handler, your program terminates with a non-zero exit code and prints the error message:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Unhandled exception caught during execution: record not found\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Raise an error\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The built-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/error/Error/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Error}} type is the default error type for most Mojo code. It carries a text message describing what went wrong, and it's the right choice for application-level error handling\'97simple, well-supported, and sufficient for the majority of use cases.\
You can raise an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with the constructor or a string literal shorthand:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # These are equivalent\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf15 "file not found"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 raise\cf13  \cf15 "file not found"\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The string literal form is a convenience\'97the compiler automatically wraps it in an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
By declaring 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you tell Mojo that a function may raise an error:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 read_file_fn\cf5 (\cf13 path\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf5 not\cf13  path\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  \cf15 "path cannot be empty"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "contents of "\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  path\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 If you need structured error data\'97like separate fields for an error code and description\'97or allocation-free errors for GPU kernels, see \ul Typed errors\ulnone  later on this page. For most application code, 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Error
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  with a descriptive string message is all you need.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Handle an error\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to detect and handle errors. The full syntax is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # Code that might raise an error\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # Runs if an error occurs\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # Runs if no error occurs\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 finally:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # Always runs, regardless of outcome\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You must include one or both of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause is optional.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 How each clause works\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 Contains code that might raise an error. If no error occurs, the entire block executes. If an error occurs, execution stops at the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raise
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  point and continues with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause (if present) or the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 Runs only when an error occurs in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block. If you provide a variable name (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except e:
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), the error is bound to that variable. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block can have only one 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 Runs only when no error occurs in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause is skipped if the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause exits via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 continue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 break
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 Runs after the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and any 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause, regardless of outcome. It executes even if another clause exits via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 continue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 break
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or by raising a new error. Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to release resources (such as file handles) that must be cleaned up regardless of whether an error occurred.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Example\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The following example demonstrates all four clauses. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 process_record()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function raises 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for different conditions, and the caller loops over a list of IDs to exercise each clause:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb21 handle_error.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 process_record\cf5 (\cf18 id\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf18 id\cf13  \cf5 <\cf13  \cf22 0\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf15 "invalid record ID: must be non-negative"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf18 id\cf13  \cf5 >\cf13  \cf22 999\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf15 "record not found"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  String\cf5 (\cf15 "record_"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 id\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 for\cf13  \cf18 id\cf13  \cf5 in\cf13  \cf5 [\cf22 5\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 0\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 1001\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 -\cf22 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 42\cf5 ]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 var\cf13  result\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 print()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 print(\cf15 "try     => id:"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 id\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 if\cf13  \cf18 id\cf13  \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf22 0\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                     \cf5 continue\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 result \cf5 =\cf13  process_record\cf5 (\cf18 id\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 if\cf13  \cf15 "invalid"\cf13  \cf5 in\cf13  String\cf5 (\cf13 e\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                     \cf5 print(\cf15 "except  => fatal:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                     \cf5 raise\cf13  e\cf5 ^\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 print(\cf15 "except  => handled:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 print(\cf15 "else    => success:"\cf5 ,\cf13  result\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 finally:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14                 \cf5 print(\cf15 "finally => done with id:"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 id\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "\\nre-raised error:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \
\cb14 try     => id: 5\cb1 \
\cb14 else    => success: record_5\cb1 \
\cb14 finally => done with id: 5\cb1 \
\
\cb14 try     => id: 0\cb1 \
\cb14 finally => done with id: 0\cb1 \
\
\cb14 try     => id: 1001\cb1 \
\cb14 except  => handled: record not found\cb1 \
\cb14 finally => done with id: 1001\cb1 \
\
\cb14 try     => id: -3\cb1 \
\cb14 except  => fatal: invalid record ID: must be non-negative\cb1 \
\cb14 finally => done with id: -3\cb1 \
\
\cb14 re-raised error: invalid record ID: must be non-negative\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Notice:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is 5: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 process_record()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  succeeds, so 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  runs, then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is 0: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 continue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  exits the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block, skipping both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Only 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  runs.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is 1001: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 process_record()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  raises an error. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause handles it and execution continues with the next iteration.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is -3: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 process_record()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  raises an "invalid" error. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause re-raises it with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raise e^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which transfers ownership of the error to the outer 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 finally
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause still runs before the error propagates. Because the re-raise exits the loop, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  42 is never processed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Re-raise an error\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To re-raise a caught error, use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raise
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#transfer-arguments-var-and-"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul transfer sigil}} (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to transfer ownership of the error value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     result \cf5 =\cf13  process_record\cf5 (-\cf22 1\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 "Logging error:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  e\cf5 ^\cf13   \cf6 # re-raise with ownership transfer\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also raise a different error from within an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Typed errors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For code that needs more than a string message\'97like standard library APIs, GPU abstractions, or situations where callers need structured error data\'97Mojo lets you define custom error types as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul structs}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Define a custom error type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, any struct can serve as an error type\'97no special base class or trait is required. However, implementing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writable}} trait is recommended so the error produces a readable message when printed or when the program terminates with an unhandled error:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf18 ValidationError\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Writable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  field\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  reason\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  writer\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf13 write\cf5 (\cf15 "ValidationError("\cf5 ,\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 field\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "): "\cf5 ,\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 reason\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/decorators/fieldwise-init/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul @fieldwise_init}} decorator generates an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method with an argument for each field, so you can construct errors like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ValidationError("username", "too short")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or with keyword arguments like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ValidationError(field="username", reason="too short")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 Typed errors work on GPUs and embedded targets as long as you avoid heap-allocated types like 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 String
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 . For more on GPU programming, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/fundamentals/"}}{\fldrslt \ul GPU fundamentals}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Raise a typed error\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To declare that a function can raise a typed error, add 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises YourErrorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to its signature:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 validate_username\cf5 (\cf13 username\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises ValidationError \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf18 len\cf5 (\cf13 username\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf22 0\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  ValidationError\cf5 (\cf13 field\cf5 =\cf15 "username"\cf5 ,\cf13  reason\cf5 =\cf15 "cannot be empty"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf18 len\cf5 (\cf13 username\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 <\cf13  \cf22 3\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  ValidationError\cf5 (\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             field\cf5 =\cf15 "username"\cf5 ,\cf13  reason\cf5 =\cf15 "must be at least 3 characters"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  username\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo functions are non-raising by default. Including 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (with or without a type) makes it a raising function. Each function can declare at most one error type. The compiler enforces this\'97if any 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raise
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement in the function body doesn't match the declared type, the program won't compile.\
If a non-raising function calls a raising function, it must handle the error locally:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # This doesn't compile \'97 validate_username() can raise\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 process_name\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 validate_username\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # This compiles \'97 the error is handled\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 process_name_safe\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf13 validate_username\cf5 (\cf13 name\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Invalid:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Catch a typed error\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to catch a typed error. The compiler automatically infers the error type from the function being called, so 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except e:
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gives you a fully typed error value\'97no casting required:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  name \cf5 =\cf13  validate_username\cf5 (\cf15 ""\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # e is a ValidationError \'97 access fields directly\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 "Error in field '"\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 field \cf5 +\cf13  \cf15 "': "\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 reason\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Which produces this output:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Error in field 'username': cannot be empty\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block can include only one 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause. Mojo doesn't support 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except ErrorType as e:
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax\'97the type is always inferred from the function being called.\
If you need to handle calls that raise different error types, use separate 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  blocks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Each try block handles one error type\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  name \cf5 =\cf13  validate_username\cf5 (\cf18 input\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # e is a ValidationError \'97 access fields directly\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 "Validation failed:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 field\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 reason\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  \cf18 file\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  open_file\cf5 (\cf13 path\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # e is a FileError \'97 match on variant\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  e \cf5 ==\cf13  FileError\cf5 .\cf13 not_found\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Missing:"\cf5 ,\cf13  path\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 If your error type implements the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul Writable}} trait, you can also pass 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 e
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  directly to 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 print()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 :\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf5 \cb20 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb20     \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # calls ValidationError.write_to()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 Which produces this output:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf13 \cb20 ValidationError(username): cannot be empty\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Representing multiple error conditions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Each function can declare only one error type in its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause. When a function can fail in multiple distinct ways, you need to represent those conditions within a single type. Mojo offers two approaches:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Enumerated error types \'97 A single struct with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variant aliases. Simpler and more efficient when you only need to distinguish between conditions.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type \'97 The standard library {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/utils/variant/Variant/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Variant}} type with separate structs per condition. More flexible when each condition needs to carry different data.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Enumerated error types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A single struct can represent all error conditions using an integer 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-values"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime
\fs32 \cb1  values}} as named constants. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_to
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method generates human-readable strings from the variant code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf18 FileError\cf5 (\cf13 Equatable\cf5 ,\cf13  ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Writable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  _variant\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf6 # Compile-time constant variants\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 comptime\cf13  not_found \cf5 =\cf13  FileError\cf5 (\cf13 _variant\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 comptime\cf13  permission_denied \cf5 =\cf13  FileError\cf5 (\cf13 _variant\cf5 =\cf22 2\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 comptime\cf13  already_exists \cf5 =\cf13  FileError\cf5 (\cf13 _variant\cf5 =\cf22 3\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 variant_name\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 if\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 _variant \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf22 1\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "not_found"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 elif\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 _variant \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf22 2\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "permission_denied"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 elif\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 _variant \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf22 3\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "already_exists"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "unknown"\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  writer\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf13 write\cf5 (\cf15 "FileError."\cf5 ,\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 variant_name\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 FileError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has a single 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field and conforms to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/comparable/Equatable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Equatable}}, the compiler auto-synthesizes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __eq__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so you can compare variants directly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 open_file\cf5 (\cf13 path\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises FileError \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf5 not\cf13  path\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  FileError\cf5 .\cf13 not_found\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  path \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf15 "/secret"\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  FileError\cf5 .\cf13 permission_denied\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "Contents of "\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  path\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can then match on specific variants in the handler:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 open_file\cf5 (\cf15 "/secret"\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  e \cf5 ==\cf13  FileError\cf5 .\cf13 not_found\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Not found:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 elif\cf13  e \cf5 ==\cf13  FileError\cf5 .\cf13 permission_denied\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Permission denied:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
