\fs48 \cf2 The 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When each error condition needs to carry different data, you can use the standard library {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/utils/variant/Variant/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Variant}} type instead of an integer-based enumeration. Define a separate struct for each condition, then combine them into a single error type with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  alias:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb21 variant_errors.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 utils \cf5 import\cf13  Variant\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf18 NotFoundError\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Writable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  path\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  writer\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf13 write\cf5 (\cf15 "file not found: "\cf5 ,\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 path\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf18 PermissionError\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Writable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  path\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  required_role\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  writer\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf13 write\cf5 (\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf15 "permission denied on "\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             self\cf5 .\cf13 path\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf15 " (requires "\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             self\cf5 .\cf13 required_role\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf15 ")"\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  FileError \cf5 =\cf13  Variant\cf5 [\cf13 NotFoundError\cf5 ,\cf13  PermissionError\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by wrapping the inner error in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 open_file\cf5 (\cf13 path\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises FileError \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  \cf5 not\cf13  path\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  FileError\cf5 (\cf13 NotFoundError\cf5 (\cf15 ""\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  path \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf15 "/secret"\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  FileError\cf5 (\cf13 PermissionError\cf5 (\cf15 "/secret"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "admin"\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 "Contents of "\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  path\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In the handler, use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .isa[T]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to test which condition occurred and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 e[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to access the inner error with its full type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 open_file\cf5 (\cf15 "/secret"\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 isa\cf5 [\cf13 NotFoundError\cf5 ]():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Not found:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 [\cf13 NotFoundError\cf5 ])\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 elif\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 isa\cf5 [\cf13 PermissionError\cf5 ]():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Access denied:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 [\cf13 PermissionError\cf5 ])\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Access denied: permission denied on /secret (requires admin)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  approach when each condition carries different fields (like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 path
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vs 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 path
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  + 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 required_role
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  above). Use the \cf5 \ul enumerated error type\cf2 \ulnone  pattern when you only need to distinguish between conditions without carrying different data per condition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 The 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 Never
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a type with no constructors\'97it can't be instantiated. This makes it useful in error-handling signatures to express two opposite guarantees:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
raises YourErrorType -> Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 The function always raises and never returns a value. This is useful for functions like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 panic()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that unconditionally signal an error.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
raises Never -> ReturnType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 The function never raises and always returns a value. This is equivalent to omitting 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  entirely.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Functions that always raise\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A function with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -> Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as its return type must never terminate with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement\'97it must raise on every code path (or loop infinitely). Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can substitute for any type, the compiler allows using such a function in place of a return value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Always raises, never returns\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 panic\cf5 (\cf13 msg\cf5 :\cf13  String\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Never\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf13 msg\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 get_value_or_panic\cf5 (\cf13 maybe\cf5 :\cf13  Optional\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ])\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  maybe\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 return\cf13  maybe\cf5 .\cf13 value\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Never substitutes for Int in this branch\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     panic\cf5 (\cf15 "value is missing"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Functions that never raise\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A function with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  guarantees at compile time that it never raises. This is equivalent to writing a plain non-raising function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # These two signatures are equivalent:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 safe_add\cf5 (\cf13 a\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises Never \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  a \cf5 +\cf13  b\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 safe_add\cf5 (\cf13 a\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  b\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  a \cf5 +\cf13  b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This equivalency is especially useful in combination with \cf5 \ul parametric raises\cf2 \ulnone , where the compiler infers 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when a function argument doesn't raise.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parametric raises\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can write {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#parameters-and-generics"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul generic functions}} that propagate the error type from a function argument to the caller. This uses a compile-time parameter for the error type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  run_action\cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     ErrorType\cf5 :\cf13  AnyType\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 ](\cf13 action\cf5 :\cf13  \cf5 def()\cf13  raises ErrorType \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises ErrorType \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  action\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ErrorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter is inferred from the function you pass in. If the function raises 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 NetworkError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_action
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  raises 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 NetworkError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . If the function raises 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ParseError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_action
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  raises 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ParseError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 fetch_data\cf5 ()\cf13  raises NetworkError \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  NetworkError\cf5 (\cf13 code\cf5 =\cf22 404\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 parse_config\cf5 ()\cf13  raises ParseError \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  ParseError\cf5 (\cf13 position\cf5 =\cf22 42\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb14 # ErrorType inferred as NetworkError\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     _ \cf5 =\cf13  run_action\cf5 (\cf13 fetch_data\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 "Network failure:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # ErrorType inferred as ParseError\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     _ \cf5 =\cf13  run_action\cf5 (\cf13 parse_config\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 "Parse failure:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If the function argument doesn't raise at all, the compiler infers 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the error type. This means 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_action
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  itself becomes non-raising, and no 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block is needed:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 get_value\cf5 ()\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  \cf22 99\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb14 # ErrorType inferred as Never \'97 no try block needed\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  result \cf5 =\cf13  run_action\cf5 (\cf13 get_value\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf15 "Got value:"\cf5 ,\cf13  result\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Which produces this output:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Got value: 99\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Typed errors and 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  interaction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Most codebases contain a mix of functions that raise, don't raise, or raise typed errors. This section covers how the styles interact and how to work with them effectively.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Wrap 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  at API boundaries\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When calling raising functions or other 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 -raising code from a function that uses typed errors, catch the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and convert it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 validate_with_error\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  value \cf5 <\cf13  \cf22 0\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  \cf15 "value cannot be negative"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  value\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 wrapped_validate\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises ValidationError \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 return\cf13  validate_with_error\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 raise\cf13  ValidationError\cf5 (\cf13 field\cf5 =\cf15 "value"\cf5 ,\cf13  reason\cf5 =\cf13 String\cf5 (\cf13 e\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Avoid bare 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  with typed errors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Using bare 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (without a type) on an function that calls typed-error functions causes type erasure\'97the compiler forgets the specific error type, even though the runtime preserves the error's identity:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Anti-pattern: bare raises erases type info at compile time\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 validate_bare_raises\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 return\cf13  validate_typed\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The caller of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 validate_bare_raises()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  receives an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , not a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ValidationError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     _ \cf5 =\cf13  validate_bare_raises\cf5 (-\cf22 5\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # e is typed as Error \'97 no field access available\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # e.field would not compile here\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 ValidationError(value): cannot be negative\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The error message still shows 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ValidationError
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  because the runtime preserves the original error's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writable}} output. But the compiler sees only 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so you lose access to structured fields. Always use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises YourErrorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to maintain type safety.\
Note that type erasure only affects uncaught errors that propagate through a bare 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function. If you catch the typed error locally, you still get full field access:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 error_caller\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         _ \cf5 =\cf13  validate_typed\cf5 (-\cf22 5\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # e is a ValidationError \'97 field access works\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Field:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 field\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "Reason:"\cf5 ,\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 reason\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Field: value Reason: cannot be negative\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Don't mix error types in a single 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  block\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can't call functions that raise different error types in the same 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block. The compiler rejects the mismatch:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 error_func\cf5 ()\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  \cf15 "something went wrong"\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 typed_func\cf5 ()\cf13  raises ValidationError \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  ValidationError\cf5 (\cf13 field\cf5 =\cf15 "x"\cf5 ,\cf13  reason\cf5 =\cf15 "invalid"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # This doesn't compile\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 mixed\cf5 ()\cf13  raises ValidationError\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         _ \cf5 =\cf13  error_func\cf5 ()\cf13    \cf6 # raises Error\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         _ \cf5 =\cf13  typed_func\cf5 ()\cf13     \cf6 # raises ValidationError\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The compiler reports:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 error: cannot call function that may raise 'Error' in a context that\cb1 \
\cb14 supports an error type of 'ValidationError'\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To call both functions, use separate 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  blocks or wrap the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 -raising function as shown in \cf5 \ul Wrap 
\fs28 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cb1  at API boundaries\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Recommendations for mixed codebases\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When working with both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and typed errors:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises YourErrorType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \'97 Always specify the error type in function signatures. Bare 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  discards type information.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use separate 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  blocks \'97 When calling functions with different error types, use nested or sequential 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  blocks to handle each type independently.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 For a complete working example of these interaction patterns, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/blob/modular/v26.2/mojo/docs/code/manual/errors/error_interaction.mojo"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul error_interaction.mojo}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Enable stack trace generation for errors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Because Mojo represents errors as alternate return values rather than stack-unwinding exceptions, stack trace collection isn't automatic. Collecting a stack trace requires heap allocation and adds runtime overhead, so it's disabled by default to keep error handling lightweight.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 important\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb19 Stack traces are a feature of the built-in 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Error
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  type only. Typed errors currently don't capture stack traces because the trace is collected inside 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Error.__init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 , and custom error structs have no equivalent hook. The examples in this section all use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Error
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  intentionally.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 By default, Mojo generates a stack trace when your program hits a segmentation fault. You can disable this by setting the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MOJO_ENABLE_STACK_TRACE_ON_CRASH
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  environment variable to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 false
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (case-insensitive).\
However, Mojo doesn't generate a stack trace when your program raises an error\'97this avoids the additional runtime overhead. To enable stack traces for raised errors, set the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MOJO_ENABLE_STACK_TRACE_ON_ERROR
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  environment variable to any value other than 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 false
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (case-insensitive).\
Keep in mind that when you compile your program with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/build/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul mojo build}}, the compiler optimizes and strips symbols by default, so often your stack trace won't be very useful.\
Consider this program:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb21 stacktrace_error.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 func2\cf5 ()\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf22 None\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf15 "Intentional error"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 func1\cf5 ()\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf22 None\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     func2\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     func1\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you compile the program with default settings and run it with the environment variable set, you'll see a stack trace without symbols:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 mojo build stacktrace_error.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb14 MOJO_ENABLE_STACK_TRACE_ON_ERROR\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf13  ./stacktrace_error\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 #0 0x... llvm::sys::PrintStackTrace(llvm::raw_ostream&, int)\cb1 \
\cb14 #1 0x... KGEN_CompilerRT_GetStackTrace\cb1 \
\cb14 #2 0x... main (./stacktrace_error+...)\cb1 \
\
\cb14 Unhandled exception caught during execution: Intentional error\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To generate a more useful stack trace, compile the program with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --debug-level full
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -g
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to include debug symbols:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 mojo build --debug-level full stacktrace_error.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb14 MOJO_ENABLE_STACK_TRACE_ON_ERROR\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf13  ./stacktrace_error\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 #0 0x... llvm::sys::PrintStackTrace(llvm::raw_ostream&, int)\cb1 \
\cb14 #1 0x... KGEN_CompilerRT_GetStackTrace\cb1 \
\cb14 #2 0x... Error.__init__[...](...) .../builtin/error.mojo:159:38\cb1 \
\cb14 #3 0x... stacktrace_error::func2() stacktrace_error.mojo:14:16\cb1 \
\cb14 #4 0x... stacktrace_error::func1() stacktrace_error.mojo:18:10\cb1 \
\cb14 #5 0x... stacktrace_error::main() stacktrace_error.mojo:22:10\cb1 \
\cb14 #6 0x... __wrap_and_execute_raising_main[...](...) .../builtin/_startup.mojo:88:18\cb1 \
\cb14 #7 0x... main .../builtin/_startup.mojo:103:4\cb1 \
\
\cb14 Unhandled exception caught during execution: Intentional error\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 With debug symbols, the trace shows the function call chain and source locations: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \uc0\u8594  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 func1()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \uc0\u8594  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 func2()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  \uc0\u8594  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error.__init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 Running your program directly with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/run/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul mojo run}} or 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 mojo
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  doesn't include debug symbols in the stack trace, even with 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 --debug-level full
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 . Use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 mojo build
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  with 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 -g
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  and run the compiled binary for symbolicated stack traces.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Capture a stack trace programmatically\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can bind the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance to a variable in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause and call its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/error/Error/#get_stack_trace"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul get_stack_trace()}} method to get the stack trace as an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional[String]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The method returns 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 None
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  if stack trace collection was disabled or unavailable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb21 stacktrace_error_capture.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 func2\cf5 ()\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf22 None\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 raise\cf13  Error\cf5 (\cf15 "Intentional error"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 func1\cf5 ()\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf22 None\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     func2\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         func1\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 except\cf13  e\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf13 e\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "-"\cf13  \cf5 *\cf13  \cf22 20\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 var\cf13  stack_trace \cf5 =\cf13  e\cf5 .\cf13 get_stack_trace\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 if\cf13  stack_trace\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 print(\cf13 stack_trace\cf5 .\cf13 value\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 print(\cf15 "No stack trace available"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When you compile with debug symbols and run with stack trace generation enabled:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 mojo build --debug-level full stacktrace_error_capture.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb14 MOJO_ENABLE_STACK_TRACE_ON_ERROR\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf13  ./stacktrace_error_capture\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Intentional error\cb1 \
\cb14 --------------------\cb1 \
\cb14 #0 0x... llvm::sys::PrintStackTrace(llvm::raw_ostream&, int)\cb1 \
\cb14 #1 0x... KGEN_CompilerRT_GetStackTrace\cb1 \
\cb14 #2 0x... Error.__init__[...](...) .../builtin/error.mojo:159:38\cb1 \
\cb14 #3 0x... stacktrace_error_capture::func2() stacktrace_error_capture.mojo:14:16\cb1 \
\cb14 #4 0x... stacktrace_error_capture::func1() stacktrace_error_capture.mojo:18:10\cb1 \
\cb14 #5 0x... stacktrace_error_capture::main() stacktrace_error_capture.mojo:23:14\cb1 \
\cb14 #6 0x... __wrap_and_execute_raising_main[...](...) .../builtin/_startup.mojo:88:18\cb1 \
\cb14 #7 0x... main .../builtin/_startup.mojo:103:4\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Without enabling stack trace generation, the output is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

