\cb14 --------------------\cb1 \
\cb14 No stack trace available\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Use a context manager\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A context manager is an object that manages resources such as files, network connections, and database connections. It provides a way to allocate resources and release them automatically when they are no longer needed, ensuring proper cleanup and preventing resource leaks even when errors occur.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 Context managers work with both typed errors and the built-in 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Error
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  type. The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 with
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  statement handles either error style transparently.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As an example, consider reading data from a file. A naive approach might look like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Obtain a file handle to read from storage\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14 f \cf5 =\cf13  \cf18 open\cf5 (\cf13 input_file\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "r"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 content \cf5 =\cf13  f\cf5 .read()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # Process the content as needed\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb14 # Close the file handle\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14 f\cf5 .\cf13 close\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Calling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/io/file/FileHandle/#close"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul close()}} releases the memory and other operating system resources associated with the opened file. If your program were to open many files without closing them, you could exhaust the resources available to your program and cause errors. The problem is even worse if you were writing to a file instead of reading from it, because the operating system might buffer the output in memory until the file is closed. If your program were to crash instead of exiting normally, that buffered data could be lost instead of being written to storage.\
The example above includes the call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 close()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but it ignores the possibility that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/io/file/FileHandle/#read"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul read()}} could raise an error, which would prevent the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 close()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from executing. To handle this scenario, you could rewrite the code to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Obtain a file handle to read from storage\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14 f \cf5 =\cf13  \cf18 open\cf5 (\cf13 input_file\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "r"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     content \cf5 =\cf13  f\cf5 .read()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Process the content as needed\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 finally:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf6 # Ensure that the file handle is closed even if read() raises an error\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     f\cf5 .\cf13 close\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/io/file/FileHandle/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul FileHandle}} struct returned by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/io/file/open/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul open()}} is a context manager. When used with Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement, a context manager ensures that the resources it manages are properly released at the end of the block, even if an error occurs. In the case of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 FileHandle
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , that means the call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 close()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  takes place automatically. So you could rewrite the example above to take advantage of the context manager (and omit the explicit call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 close()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 with\cf13  \cf18 open\cf5 (\cf13 input_file\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "r"\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 as\cf13  f\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     content \cf5 =\cf13  f\cf5 .read()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Process the content as needed\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement also allows you to use multiple context managers within the same code block. As an example, the following code opens one text file, reads its entire content, converts it to upper case, and then writes the result to a different file:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 with\cf13  \cf18 open\cf5 (\cf13 input_file\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "r"\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 as\cf13  f_in\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf18 open\cf5 (\cf13 output_file\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "w"\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 as\cf13  f_out\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     input_text \cf5 =\cf13  f_in\cf5 .read()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     output_text \cf5 =\cf13  input_text\cf5 .\cf13 upper\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     f_out\cf5 .\cf13 write\cf5 (\cf13 output_text\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 FileHandle
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is perhaps the most commonly used context manager. Other examples of context managers in the Mojo standard library are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/tempfile/tempfile/NamedTemporaryFile/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul NamedTemporaryFile}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/tempfile/tempfile/TemporaryDirectory/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul TemporaryDirectory}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/utils/lock/BlockingScopedLock/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul BlockingScopedLock}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/testing/testing/assert_raises/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul assert_raises}}. You can also create your own custom context managers, as described in \cf5 \ul Write a custom context manager\cf2 \ulnone  below.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Write a custom context manager\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Writing a custom context manager is a matter of defining a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul struct}} that implements two special dunder methods ("double underscore" methods): 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __enter__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
__enter__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is called by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement to enter the runtime context. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __enter__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method should initialize any state necessary for the context and return the context manager.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
__exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is called when the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block completes execution, even if the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block terminates with a call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 continue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 break
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method should release any resources associated with the context. After the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method returns, the context manager is destroyed.\uc0\u8232 If the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block raises an error, then the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method runs before any error processing occurs (that is, before it is caught by a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  structure or your program terminates). If you'd like to define conditional processing for error conditions in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block, you can implement an overloaded version of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that takes an error argument. For more information, see \cf5 \ul Define a conditional 
\fs28 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cb1  method\cf2 \ulnone  and \cf5 \ul Handle typed errors in 
\fs28 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \ulnone  below.\uc0\u8232 For context managers that don't need to release resources or perform other actions on termination, you are not required to implement an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. In that case the context manager is destroyed automatically after the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block completes execution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here is an example of implementing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Timer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  context manager, which prints the amount of time spent executing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb21 context_mgr.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 import\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 sys\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 import\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 time\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf13  \cf18 Timer\cf5 (\cf13 ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 var\cf13  start_time\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf13 start_time \cf5 =\cf13  \cf22 0\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 __enter__\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Self\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf13 start_time \cf5 =\cf13  Int\cf5 (\cf13 time\cf5 .\cf13 perf_counter_ns\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 return\cf13  self\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf18 __exit__\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         end_time \cf5 =\cf13  time\cf5 .\cf13 perf_counter_ns\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         elapsed_time_ms \cf5 =\cf13  \cf18 round\cf5 (\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             Float64\cf5 (\cf13 end_time \cf5 -\cf13  UInt\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 .\cf13 start_time\cf5 ))\cf13  \cf5 /\cf13  \cf22 1e6\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 3\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Elapsed time:"\cf5 ,\cf13  elapsed_time_ms\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 "milliseconds"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14     \cf5 with\cf13  Timer\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Beginning execution"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         time\cf5 .\cf13 sleep\cf5 (\cf22 1.0\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 if\cf13  \cf18 len\cf5 (\cf13 sys\cf5 .\cf13 argv\cf5 ())\cf13  \cf5 >\cf13  \cf22 1\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 raise\cf13  \cf15 "simulated error"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         time\cf5 .\cf13 sleep\cf5 (\cf22 1.0\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 "Ending execution"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

