\fs48 \cf2 Define a conditional 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  method\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When creating a context manager, you can implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__(self)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  form of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to handle completion of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement under all circumstances including errors. However, you have the option of additionally implementing an overloaded version that is invoked instead when an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  occurs in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf18 __exit__\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  error\cf5 :\cf13  Error\cf5 )\cf13  raises \cf5 ->\cf13  Bool\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Given the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that occurred as an argument, the method can do any of the following:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Return 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to suppress the error.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Return 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 False
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to re-raise the error.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Raise a new error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The following is an example of a context manager that suppresses only a certain error condition and propagates all others:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb21 conditional_context_mgr.mojo\
