\fs48 \cf2 Handle typed errors in 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__(self, error: Error)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  overload handles only 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values. To handle typed errors, implement a generic 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __exit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method with a compile-time error type parameter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  __exit__\cf5 [\cf13 ErrType\cf5 :\cf13  AnyType\cf5 ](\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  err\cf5 :\cf13  ErrType\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Bool\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This method receives the typed error directly, preserving its full type information. You can use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/reflection/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul reflection}} to inspect the error type at compile time. For example, you can:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
get_type_name[ErrType]()
